WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:16.140
Mary did not miss her mother at all.

2
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.441
After all she had not really known her mother.

3
00:00:20.521 --> 00:00:24.322
So now, after the death of her parents, she thought only of herself.

4
00:00:25.543 --> 00:00:29.804
She hoped she was going to live with nice people who would let her do whatever she wanted.

5
00:00:33.576 --> 00:00:37.098
At first she was taken to the home of the local English minister.

6
00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:42.400
He had five children who were always quarreling and snatching toys from each other.

7
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.121
Mary hated staying at their crowded, messy house.

8
00:00:47.222 --> 00:00:52.604
She was so disagreeable to the children that, after the first day or two, nobody would play with

9
00:00:52.664 --> 00:00:57.166
her. One day Mary was playing by herself under a tree.

10
00:00:58.206 --> 00:01:02.488
She was pretending to make a garden, just as she had been doing the day cholera broke out.

11
00:01:03.744 --> 00:01:07.186
One of the little boys, Basil, stood nearby watching her.

12
00:01:08.247 --> 00:01:12.589
"You are going to be sent home at the end of the week," Basil said to her.

13
00:01:12.609 --> 00:01:15.711
"And we're all glad about it."

14
00:01:15.731 --> 00:01:19.473
"I'm glad too," answered Mary.

15
00:01:19.493 --> 00:01:20.913
"Where is home?"

16
00:01:22.134 --> 00:01:23.895
"You don't know where home is?"

17
00:01:24.455 --> 00:01:25.816
Basil said scornfully.

18
00:01:25.836 --> 00:01:28.277
"It's England of course.

19
00:01:28.897 --> 00:01:31.839
You are going to live with your uncle, Mr.

20
00:01:32.099 --> 00:01:33.360
Archibald Craven."

21
00:01:34.364 --> 00:01:35.185
Mary frowned.

22
00:01:35.205 --> 00:01:37.306
"I don't know anything about him."

23
00:01:37.326 --> 00:01:40.028
"Of course you don't.

24
00:01:40.429 --> 00:01:43.110
You don't know anything," said Basil.

25
00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:47.113
"But I heard Father and Mother talking about him.

26
00:01:48.194 --> 00:01:52.817
He lives in a big, old house called Misselthwaite Manor.

27
00:01:53.918 --> 00:01:55.319
No one goes near him.

28
00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.361
He's a hunchback and he's horrid."

29
00:01:58.381 --> 00:02:01.664
"I don't believe you."

30
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.562
Mary turned her back and stuck her fingers in her ears because she didn't want to hear any

31
00:02:07.642 --> 00:02:11.143
more. But she thought it over a great deal afterward.

32
00:02:12.183 --> 00:02:14.063
What sort of place was she going to?

33
00:02:14.644 --> 00:02:16.004
What would her uncle be like?

34
00:02:17.104 --> 00:02:18.765
What was a hunchback anyway?

35
00:02:19.285 --> 00:02:20.365
What did one look like?

36
00:02:22.226 --> 00:02:27.408
Mary made the long voyage to England under the care of an officer's wife who was taking her

37
00:02:27.468 --> 00:02:28.648
children to boarding school.

38
00:02:29.676 --> 00:02:34.197
The officer's wife was too busy with her own little boy and girl to pay much attention to

39
00:02:34.237 --> 00:02:38.499
Mary. In London the officer's wife handed Mary over to Mrs.

40
00:02:38.559 --> 00:02:42.020
Medlock, the woman who was the housekeeper at Misselthwaite Manor.

41
00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.681
"My word!

42
00:02:44.721 --> 00:02:46.221
She's a plain little thing!"

43
00:02:46.761 --> 00:02:47.621
exclaimed Mrs.

44
00:02:47.661 --> 00:02:51.162
Medlock. "And we'd heard her mother was a beauty."

45
00:02:51.182 --> 00:02:56.064
"Perhaps she will improve as she grows older," said the officer's wife.

46
00:02:57.024 --> 00:03:01.585
The two women thought that Mary could not hear them because she was standing a little apart from

47
00:03:01.625 --> 00:03:03.906
them, but she heard every word.

48
00:03:05.347 --> 00:03:06.067
She thought Mrs.

49
00:03:06.127 --> 00:03:09.648
Medlock was the most disagreeable- looking woman she had ever seen.

50
00:03:10.708 --> 00:03:13.369
The next day they had to catch the train to Yorkshire.

51
00:03:14.369 --> 00:03:18.991
When they walked through the station, Mary tried to keep as far away from her as possible.

52
00:03:20.031 --> 00:03:22.412
She did not want anyone to think that she was Mrs.

53
00:03:22.512 --> 00:03:23.412
Medlock's daughter.

54
00:03:24.944 --> 00:03:27.045
Once they were settled on the train, Mrs.

55
00:03:27.105 --> 00:03:30.627
Medlock said, "Do you know anything about your uncle?"

56
00:03:30.647 --> 00:03:33.449
"No," Mary replied.

57
00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:36.070
"Well," said Mrs.

58
00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:40.793
Medlock. "I suppose you should be told something to prepare yourself.

59
00:03:41.953 --> 00:03:44.074
You are going to a strange place."

60
00:03:45.715 --> 00:03:46.716
Mary said nothing.

61
00:03:47.256 --> 00:03:50.718
She didn't care what this awful woman had to say about her uncle's house.

62
00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:55.672
"The house is six hundred years old," said Mrs.

63
00:03:55.732 --> 00:04:02.434
Medlock. "It's on the edge of the moor, and there are nearly a hundred rooms, though most of

64
00:04:02.494 --> 00:04:03.975
them are closed up and locked."

65
00:04:05.696 --> 00:04:06.876
Mary began to listen.

66
00:04:07.396 --> 00:04:11.077
It sounded so different from India, and anything new interested her.

67
00:04:12.138 --> 00:04:13.978
But she did not want to look interested.

68
00:04:13.998 --> 00:04:18.200
"Mr. Craven has a crooked back," Mrs.

69
00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:19.100
Medlock went on.

70
00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:26.295
"He was a sour young man, and did nothing good with his money and his big house until

71
00:04:26.355 --> 00:04:27.055
he married."

72
00:04:28.535 --> 00:04:30.176
Now Mary was really interested.

73
00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:33.497
She'd never thought of a hunchback as married.

74
00:04:33.517 --> 00:04:39.780
"His wife was a sweet, pretty thing, and he would have done anything for her.

75
00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.582
People said she married him for his money, but she didn't.

76
00:04:44.162 --> 00:04:47.803
And when she died "She died?"

77
00:04:48.324 --> 00:04:49.224
Mary exclaimed.

78
00:04:50.344 --> 00:04:54.667
She remembered a fairy tale about a poor hunchback and a beautiful princess.

79
00:04:55.767 --> 00:04:57.528
Suddenly she felt sorry for Mr.

80
00:04:57.548 --> 00:05:01.210
Craven. "Yes, she died," said Mrs.

81
00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:04.893
Medlock. "And that made him even stranger.

82
00:05:05.393 --> 00:05:07.194
He cares about nobody.

83
00:05:07.554 --> 00:05:08.955
He won't see people.

84
00:05:10.896 --> 00:05:16.499
Most of the time he travels, but when he's at Misselthwaite Manor, he shuts himself up in the

85
00:05:16.559 --> 00:05:17.260
west wing.

86
00:05:18.464 --> 00:05:22.287
He won't let anybody near him but his old servant, Pitcher."

87
00:05:23.308 --> 00:05:24.609
Mrs. Medlock was right.

88
00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:27.692
Mary was going to a strange place indeed.
